WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Toyota used to be ahead of its time.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.200
When General Motors didn’t think&nbsp;
there was a market for small cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.040
Toyota introduced small, compact cars&nbsp;
to America beginning in the fifties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.280
and eventually, overtook GM to&nbsp;
become the world’s largest automaker.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.480
Research firms have rated&nbsp;
Toyota and its luxury line Lexus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.200
among the top automotive brands for years.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.600
However, the glory days may be over.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.720
Like other automakers, Toyota struggled&nbsp;
during the pandemic with chip shortages.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.160
In the fall of 2021 - it made 40% fewer cars&nbsp;
and trucks as a result of supply chain issues.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.960
But the problems may not end with the pandemic.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.720
On top of everything else, Toyota’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.440
progress toward developing electric&nbsp;
vehicles has been incredibly slow.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:47.360
It still does not have a pure electric vehicle&nbsp;
on the road, those powered only by batteries.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.960
It does have one in the pipeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.520
The yet-to-be-released bZ4X was&nbsp;
only revealed in the fall of 2021.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.480
Toyota is lagging behind despite having promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.520
plans for the clean energy&nbsp;
car market two decades ago.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.960
Toyota wowed the world with the first&nbsp;
commercial hybrid, the Prius, in 1997.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.840
Hybrids combine gas engines with electric motors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.120
so they use less fuel than&nbsp;
traditional gasoline vehicles.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.680
And then, Toyota started producing an electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.480
RAV4 in 2012 - the world’s only&nbsp;
all-electric SUV at the time.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.400
To make it happen, it partnered with&nbsp;
a little California car company.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.200
Tesla.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.720
Toyota’s president was blown&nbsp;
away by Tesla’s roadster.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.404
Akio Toyoda said he felt&nbsp;
“...the wind of the future.”

00:01:28.404 --> 00:01:29.920
You can put the full quote on-screen: “To put it&nbsp;
simply, I felt the wind, the wind of the future.”

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.640
The two companies even&nbsp;
shared a factory for a while.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.600
However, Toyota discontinued the RAV4 EV&nbsp;
after only two years. It had a limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.040
customer base as it was sold only in California,&nbsp;
and there were issues with long charging times.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.320
Toyota and Tesla parted ways&nbsp;
in 2014 and became rivals.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.440
Toyota’s president has taken shots at Elon Musk&nbsp;
as recently as two years ago - saying Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.640
may have a recipe but it lacks a kitchen&nbsp;
and a chef - which Toyota argues it has.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.080
Tesla and Toyota have polar&nbsp;
opposite EV strategies.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.480
Tesla has focused solely on developing&nbsp;
electric vehicles that are battery-powered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.800
known as Battery Electric Vehicles (BEVs).

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.800
Toyota has chosen to focus on three other types of&nbsp;
electrified vehicles that are NOT purely electric.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.800
Hybrids, plug-in hybrids which can be&nbsp;
plugged in to extend driving range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.480
and hydrogen-powered fuel-cell cars which Elon&nbsp;
Musk has dismissed as “fool-cell” vehicles.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.200
Fuel cell vehicles use hydrogen gas which&nbsp;
mixes with oxygen to produce electricity.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.320
Musk has said it’s difficult to&nbsp;
make hydrogen and store it in a car.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.880
Toyota offers one hydrogen model, the Mirai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.200
but seems to have a special thing for hybrids&nbsp;
and plug-in hybrids, which made up basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.760
all of its half a million electrified&nbsp;
vehicles sales in North America in 2021.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.840
Although plug-in hybrids can be powered&nbsp;
by battery they can’t travel very far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:01.040
without relying on the gas engine. The Prius Prime&nbsp;
can only go a mere 25 miles or 40 km in EV mode.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.280
Some see hybrids as a stepping stone for those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.560
aren’t quite ready to make the&nbsp;
leap to a pure electric vehicle.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.200
However, the future appears to be all-electric.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.280
Battery electric vehicles are&nbsp;
outselling plug-in hybrids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.720
according to data compiled by&nbsp;
the International Energy Agency.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.640
And by the way, this was tabulated before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.160
the insane prices currently&nbsp;
hurting drivers at the pumps.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.080
So, why has Toyota been so reluctant&nbsp;
to embrace battery-electric cars?

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.680
In 2020, Toyota’s president Akio Toyoda&nbsp;
said it’s too early to focus solely on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.040
battery-powered electric vehicles…in response&nbsp;
to his country’s push to ban gas-powered cars.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.040
The grandson of Toyota’s founder argued&nbsp;
Japan would run out of electricity in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.760
the summertime if all cars ran on electric power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.000
and pointed out upgrading infrastructure&nbsp;
would cost hundreds of billions of dollars.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.200
It’s true that transitioning to a 100% electric&nbsp;
vehicle fleet will require billions of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.080
of investment in the grid - one study&nbsp;
put it at up to $125 billion for the U.S.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.600
But that may be inevitable.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.480
Shuli Goodman of the Linux Foundation project&nbsp;
LF Energy said to the Washington Post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.240
“The grid of the future isn’t going to be&nbsp;
a grid at all. It will be more like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.120
Internet,”...with power generation happening&nbsp;
all over the place. “Something like a forest.”

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.200
Advocates of EVs are also pushing&nbsp;
renewable energy to charge an electric car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.400
And charging at night when&nbsp;
electricity demand is lower.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.320
But expanding the grid isn’t the only&nbsp;
issue concerning the head of Toyota.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.800
Akio Toyoda has said going all-in on&nbsp;
EVs in Japan could cost millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.520
jobs. Battery-electric vehicles have fewer&nbsp;
parts and require fewer laborers to make.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.720
EVs would also shrink the&nbsp;
third-party supplier ecosystem.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.200
Although it seems Toyota is finally&nbsp;
pivoting toward EVs as it plans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.080
introduce 30 all-electric models&nbsp;
by 2030 - looks can be deceiving.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.000
The company is still not&nbsp;
abandoning its love of hybrids!

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.840
Out of the 8 million electrified vehicles Toyota&nbsp;
hopes to sell by 2030, only 3.5 million will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.760
be purely electric - a far cry from the 20&nbsp;
million Tesla aims to sell annually by 2030.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.880
And we look at the specs of Toyota’s upcoming&nbsp;
bZ4X - it’s less impressive than a Tesla.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.200
Delivering less range and taking more time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.640
go zero to 60 miles an hour&nbsp;
than any of Tesla’s models.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.440
But of course, price matters. We don’t yet&nbsp;
know what Toyota’s electric SUV will cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.560
and if it will be significantly less than a Tesla.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.120
Some say Tesla is the Apple of cars.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.040
When Nokia, Ericsson, and Motorola&nbsp;
all missed the smartphone revolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.880
they never caught up to Apple.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:31.200
Toyota could find itself in the same boat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.960
if it remains reluctant to go&nbsp;
all-in on battery-powered EVs.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.480
More and more EVs are being sold every year.

